# Grand Theft Auto V (PPSA04263) — DISCOVERY route for the in-game pause menu.
#
# NOT a progression route. Its only job is to open the pause menu after gameplay is reached and then
# walk it slowly enough that a screenshot series can be used to READ the real layout, so the actual
# settings route can be written from capture rather than from memory of the retail game. Delete or
# supersede it once reach-graphics-settings.pad is established.
#
# Why a discovery pass instead of writing the route directly: this project has already lost time to
# assumed input mappings (Asterix's Skip was Triangle, not Cross, and a Cross-only mash sat through
# 215 s of cutscene looking exactly like a renderer stall). GTA V's pause menu is tab-based and the
# tab ORDER and the settings sub-page position are exactly the kind of detail that is cheap to
# measure once and expensive to guess repeatedly.
#
# Timing note, carried from reach-story-mode.pad: the flip rate is NOT constant. Boot and the
# IoStore-bound load make the first 600 flips take ~59 s, while gameplay is reached around f5597 at
# ~165 s. Frames therefore arrive much faster once the world is up, so the post-gameplay section
# below is spaced widely in FLIPS to stay spaced in SECONDS.

# --- Reach gameplay: identical to reach-story-mode.pad -------------------------------------------
# Kept verbatim rather than factored out, because the .pad format has no include and a silent drift
# between the two copies would be diagnosed as a menu change rather than as an edit.
f600-620:cross
f800-820:cross
f1000-1020:cross
f1200-1220:cross
f1400-1420:cross
f1600-1620:cross
f1800-1820:cross
f2000-2020:cross
f2200-2220:cross
f2400-2420:cross
f2600-2620:r1
f2700-2720:r1
f2800-2820:r1
f2880-2900:r1
f3000-3020:cross
f3300-3320:cross
f3600-3620:cross
f4000-4020:cross
f4400-4420:cross
f4800-4820:cross
f5200-5220:cross
f5600-5620:cross
f6000-6020:cross

# --- Settle, then OPEN THE PAUSE MENU -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# EVERYTHING PAST GAMEPLAY IS ANCHORED IN SECONDS, NOT FLIPS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
#
# Measured on the first discovery run (2026-08-21): this title reaches gameplay quickly -- the last
# flip-anchored press, f3600, fired at 85.6 s and the tutorial text "The Radar shows your position
# within the world." is on screen -- and then THE FLIP RATE COLLAPSES. Over the remaining 315 s of a
# 401 s run, fewer than 400 further flips occurred (~1.3/s), against ~42/s during boot. The run
# summary agrees from the other side: 1,295 distinct frames of 11,932 published, "9% of the run
# active".
#
# So a flip-anchored press placed after gameplay may never fire at all. The first version of this
# file put the Options press at f7000 and the tab walk out to f10400; NONE of them executed, and the
# run looked like a route that had simply failed. It had not -- those anchors were unreachable.
#
# pad.cpp:207: a leading 'f' selects flips, a leading 'p' selects pad reads, and ANYTHING ELSE IS
# SECONDS. Wall-clock keeps advancing while flips stall, so seconds are the only anchor that behaves
# predictably once the world is up. Boot stays flip-anchored, where the flip rate is high and the
# wall-clock varies with disk load.

120-122:options

# --- Walk the tab bar one step at a time ---------------------------------------------------------
# 20 s between presses, which at a 2 s screenshot cadence gives ~10 frames per tab -- enough that a
# tab cannot fall between samples. Right-only: if the bar clamps at its last tab extra presses are
# harmless, whereas mixing L1 and R1 leaves the series ambiguous about which tab is shown.
140-142:r1
160-162:r1
180-182:r1
200-202:r1
220-222:r1
240-242:r1
260-262:r1
280-282:r1

# Deliberately NO Cross after the menu opens. Entering a sub-page would change what the later
# screenshots show without recording which tab was entered, and this run exists to produce an
# unambiguous tab-by-tab series.
